CAT = "iespLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
# Units 1 and 2 of the SOLAS Safe Pass programme: introduction to site safety, legislation and site safety.
# Safety, Health and Welfare at Work Act 2005 (ss. 8, 13, 19, 20, 25, 27) and the Safety, Health and
# Welfare at Work (Construction) Regulations 2013 (Regs 4, 10, 19, 23, 25, 26, Schedule 4).
Q = [
('Which State body enforces health and safety law on Irish construction sites?',
 'The Health and Safety Authority', ['SOLAS, which runs Safe Pass', 'The local county council', 'The Construction Industry Federation'],
 'The Health and Safety Authority (HSA) inspects workplaces and enforces the 2005 Act and its regulations. SOLAS runs the Safe Pass programme but does not enforce site safety law.'),
('Which law sets out the general duties of employers and employees at work in Ireland?',
 'The Safety, Health and Welfare at Work Act 2005', ['The Building Control Act 2007', 'The Organisation of Working Time Act 1997', 'The Planning and Development Act 2000 and its rules'],
 'The 2005 Act is the main Irish health and safety statute. The Construction Regulations 2013 are made under it and add duties specific to building sites.'),
('Which regulations set the specific health and safety duties for construction work in Ireland?',
 'The Construction Regulations 2013', ['The Building Regulations 1997', 'The Fire Services Act 1981', 'The Planning and Development Regulations 2001'],
 'The Safety, Health and Welfare at Work (Construction) Regulations 2013 cover project supervisors, Safe Pass, site safety representatives, excavations and other construction duties.'),
('Under the Construction Regulations 2013, which of these workers must hold a valid Safe Pass card?',
 'A general construction operative', ['A clerk in the site office', 'A client visiting on a site tour', 'An architect at a site meeting'],
 'Schedule 4 applies to craft and general construction workers, on-site security workers and others the Minister prescribes. Office staff, supervised visitors and design professionals are not covered.'),
('Apart from craft and general workers, which group named in the 2013 regulations needs Safe Pass?',
 'On-site security staff', ['Canteen and catering staff', 'Visiting sales representatives', 'Show house visitors'],
 'Regulation 4(2) names persons undertaking on-site security work alongside craft and general construction workers. HSA guidance lists canteen and sales staff as not needing it.'),
('Who on a site must coordinate arrangements so that workers hold valid Safe Pass cards?',
 'The PSCS', ['The client', 'The architect', 'The local authority'],
 'Regulation 19 makes the project supervisor for the construction stage (PSCS) coordinate arrangements so that every worker who needs one holds a valid safety awareness card.'),
('Besides the PSCS, who must make sure each worker under their direct control holds a valid Safe Pass card?',
 'Their contractor or employer', ["The worker's trade union", 'The owners of the next-door site', "The HSA's local inspector"],
 'Regulation 25 places the duty on every contractor or other person under whose direct control people work. The PSCS coordinates; the employer must check its own workers.'),
('What does PSCS stand for on an Irish construction project?',
 'Project Supervisor Construction Stage', ['Principal Site Contractor for Safety', 'Public Safety Compliance Service', 'Project Safety and Construction Supervisor'],
 'The PSCS is appointed by the client to manage and coordinate health and safety during the construction stage. The PSDP does the same at the design stage.'),
('Which duty holder is responsible for health and safety during the design of a project?',
 'The PSDP', ['The PSCS', 'The site safety representative', 'The safety officer'],
 'The project supervisor for the design process (PSDP) coordinates designers and prepares the preliminary safety and health plan before work starts on site.'),
('Under section 13 of the 2005 Act, which of these is a duty of every employee?',
 'Report defects that could endanger anyone', ["Write the site's safety statement", "Appoint the contractor's own safety officer", 'Notify the whole project to the HSA'],
 'Section 13 requires employees to report defects in the workplace, equipment or system of work as soon as practicable. The safety statement and notification are duties of the employer and client.'),
('What does the 2005 Act require of an employee about alcohol and drugs?',
 'Not to be under their influence to a dangerous extent', ['Only to avoid alcohol before the noon break', 'To declare any drink taken the night before', 'Nothing, as it is purely an employer matter'],
 'Section 13(1)(b) says an employee must not be under the influence of an intoxicant to the extent that they endanger themselves or anyone else at work.'),
('Under the 2005 Act, an employer may reasonably require an employee to do what about intoxicants?',
 'Submit to an appropriate test', ['Sign a weekly sobriety declaration', 'Pay for their own medical check', 'Stay off site for a full day'],
 'Section 13(1)(c) says an employee must, if reasonably required, submit to appropriate, reasonable and proportionate tests for intoxicants under the supervision of a registered medical practitioner.'),
('A worker skips the safety training their employer reasonably requires. Which duty have they broken?',
 'To attend required safety training', ['To prepare the site risk assessment', 'To notify the HSA of the project', 'To appoint a safety representative'],
 'Section 13(1)(f) says employees must attend the training, and undergo any assessment, that the employer reasonably requires on safety, health and welfare.'),
('A worker plays a practical joke with a compressed air line. Which employee duty does this break?',
 'Not to engage in improper conduct', ['To report defects as soon as possible', 'To attend required safety training', 'To cooperate with consultation'],
 'Section 13(1)(e) bans improper conduct or behaviour likely to endanger the employee or anyone else. Horseplay with air lines has caused serious internal injuries.'),
('What is a safety statement?',
 "The employer's written plan for managing safety", ["A worker's signed account of an accident", "The HSA's yearly list of the sites it will inspect", 'A certificate issued at the end of Safe Pass'],
 'Section 20 requires every employer to prepare a written safety statement, based on its hazard identification and risk assessment, setting out how safety will be managed.'),
('On what must an employer base its safety statement?',
 'Its hazard identification and risk assessment', ["Last year's accident figures and nothing else", "The client's design drawings for the job", 'The notes handed out on the Safe Pass course'],
 'Section 20 says the safety statement is based on the hazards identified and the risk assessment carried out under section 19 of the 2005 Act.'),
('Under the 2005 Act, who may select a safety representative at a workplace?',
 'The employees, from among themselves', ['The HSA inspector for the area', "The client's architect on the project", "The employer's insurance company"],
 'Section 25 lets employees select and appoint a safety representative from among their number to represent them in consultation with the employer.'),
('On a site where more than 20 people normally work at one time, which role must the PSCS help appoint?',
 'A site safety representative', ['A first aid instructor for the site', 'A Garda liaison officer', 'A county council planning inspector'],
 "Regulation 23 requires the PSCS to facilitate the appointment of a site safety representative from among the contractors' employees where more than 20 persons are normally employed at any one time."),
('Which of these may a site safety representative do?',
 'Investigate accidents and dangerous occurrences', ['Issue prohibition notices to the contractors', 'Fine contractors for unsafe practices', 'Close the site by law until it is made safe again'],
 'Regulation 23 lets a site safety representative investigate accidents, dangerous occurrences and complaints and accompany inspectors. Prohibition notices are issued only by HSA inspectors.'),
('A contractor normally has more than 20 people on one site. Whom must it appoint in writing?',
 'A competent safety officer', ['A trade union shop steward', 'A second, separate PSCS', 'A full-time occupational nurse'],
 'Regulation 26 requires a contractor with more than 20 persons on a site, or 30 engaged in construction work, to appoint one or more competent safety officers in writing.'),
('A worker raises a safety complaint and is then moved to worse shifts. Which part of the 2005 Act protects them?',
 'The protection against penalisation', ["The employee's duty to report defects", 'The rules on the safety statement', 'The rule on testing for intoxicants'],
 'Section 27 says an employer must not penalise or threaten an employee for making a complaint or representation about safety, health or welfare.'),
('An HSA inspector finds workers on an unguarded roof edge with a serious risk of a fall. Which notice directs that the work stop?',
 'A prohibition notice', ['An improvement notice', 'A contravention report', 'A notice of intended prosecution'],
 'Under section 67 of the 2005 Act a prohibition notice is served where an activity involves or is likely to involve a risk of serious personal injury. It prohibits the activity until the matters giving rise to the risk are remedied.'),
('A contractor receives an HSA improvement notice about poor welfare facilities. What must the contractor do?',
 'Put it right within the stated time', ['Close the site at once for a week', "Withdraw the workers' Safe Pass cards", 'Apply for new planning permission'],
 'Under section 66 of the 2005 Act an improvement notice states the contravention found and directs the person to remedy it by a date specified in the notice.'),
('A client plans construction work lasting longer than 30 working days. What must the client do?',
 'Notify the HSA in writing', ['Inform the local Garda station', 'Apply for a new Safe Pass', 'Tell the neighbours in writing'],
 'Regulation 10 requires the client to notify the HSA where work will last longer than 30 working days or exceed 500 person days.'),
('Above how many person days of work must a client notify the HSA even if the job is short?',
 '500', ['100', '250', '1,000'],
 'Regulation 10 sets two triggers for notification: more than 30 working days or more than 500 person days of work.'),
('What does a worker normally receive on arriving at a new site, on top of Safe Pass?',
 'A site-specific safety induction', ['A second Safe Pass course for that site', 'A full medical examination', 'A personal visit from the HSA'],
 "Safe Pass is general awareness. Each site's induction covers its own hazards, rules and emergency arrangements; Regulation 25 makes it a legal requirement for some workers who are exempt from Safe Pass, and sites give it to everyone in practice."),
("Does holding a Safe Pass card replace the employer's duty to train a worker for a task?",
 'No, the employer must still train them', ['Yes, the card covers every task on site', 'Yes, for workers over the age of 25', 'Only for craft workers, not operatives'],
 "HSA guidance says Safe Pass is a general awareness programme and does not remove the employer's duty under the 2005 Act to give the information, instruction and training a job needs."),
("Under section 8 of the 2005 Act, the employer's general duty to keep workers safe applies how far?",
 'So far as is reasonably practicable', ['Only where the HSA has already visited', 'Only on sites that have appointed a PSCS', 'Only for permanent, full-time employees'],
 'Section 8 places the general duty on employers to ensure, so far as is reasonably practicable, the safety, health and welfare at work of each of their employees.'),
]
